"About 73,000 years ago, someone picked up a piece of ochre and drew a pattern of crossing lines on a stone. A small flake of that stone was found in a cave in South Africa, and it is the oldest drawing we know of. Nobody can say what the lines meant. But the person who drew them did what every child with a crayon still does: pressed a point against a surface and moved it. That is all a line is. The Getty Museum's teaching pages describe a line as a path made by a point moving in space. A line can change in width, in direction, and in length, and lines often mark the edges of things. A line that follows the outer edge of a shape is called an outline. Lines that travel along the edges and surfaces of an object, so that it looks solid, are called contour lines. A line also records how it was made. Press harder and the line gets thicker and darker. Hold your hand steady and the edge is clean, and let it tremble and the edge turns rough. Artists call these qualities, thick or thin, hard or soft, dark or light, the line quality. In Chinese painting there is a way of working called baimiao, which uses lines alone, with no color. A skilled painter can show weight, movement, and texture through nothing but the thickness, firmness, and spacing of the lines. Direction matters too. The Getty pages say that horizontal lines suggest rest, because things that lie flat on the ground are at rest. Vertical lines often give a sense of height. Diagonal lines can give a feeling of movement, because a thing that leans looks as if it might fall. Notice the careful words: suggest, often, can. These are habits of seeing that artists use. They are not rules that every picture must follow. Lines can do two more jobs. They can close up to make shapes. Geometric shapes are the ones with names from math class, such as squares, triangles, and circles. Organic shapes are irregular, like leaves, clouds, and puddles. Lines can also make shading. Many thin parallel lines set close together look like a gray tone from a distance. This is called hatching, and when a second set of lines crosses the first, it is cross-hatching. That is one way to draw the light and shade from Lesson 3. In 1925 the painter Paul Klee opened a small book for his students with these words: an active line on a walk, moving freely, without goal. People often shorten this to taking a line for a walk. It is a good way to begin. Put your pencil down, let it travel, and then look at what the line shows. Was it fast or slow? Heavy or light? Sure or unsure? Your line has already said something about you.": "1b23e5d08c72",
